              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The word **comet** has both a modern scientific definition and a fascinating literal meaning based on its ancient origins. \n\n### 1. Modern Scientific Definition\nIn astronomy, a **comet** is a small, icy celestial body that orbits the Sun. Comets are made of frozen gases, rocks, and dust (often described as \"dirty snowballs\"). \n\nAs a comet\u2019s orbit brings it close to the Sun, it heats up and begins to outgas. This process creates a glowing, temporary atmosphere around the comet\u2019s core called a **coma**, as well as a spectacular, streaming **tail** of gas and dust that points away from the Sun.\n\n### 2. Etymology (Word History)\nThe literal meaning of the word *comet* is **\"long-haired star.\"**\n\nHere is how the word evolved:\n* **Ancient Greek:** The word comes from the Greek word ***kom\u0113t\u0113s*** (\u03ba\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae\u03c4\u03b7\u03c2), which literally means **\"wearing long hair\"** or **\"long-haired.\"**\n* **The Phrase:** Ancient Greeks used the term ***ast\u0113r kom\u0113t\u0113s*** (\u1f00\u03c3\u03c4\u1f74\u03c1 \u03ba\u03bf\u03bc\u03ae\u03c4\u03b7\u03c2) to describe these objects in the night sky, which translates directly to **\"long-haired star.\"** The root word is *kom\u0113* (\u03ba\u03cc\u03bc\u03b7), meaning \"hair of the head.\" This name was given because the long, glowing tail of a comet looks like flowing locks of hair trailing behind a star.\n* **Evolution of the spelling:** Over time, the phrase was shortened to just *kom\u0113t\u0113s*. This was adopted into Latin as ***cometa*** (or *cometes*), transitioned into Old French as ***comete***, and finally entered the English language in the 12th century as ***comet***.\n\nEven today, the astronomical symbol for a comet (\u2604) represents a small disc with three trailing, hairlike extensions, keeping this ancient \"hairy star\" connection alive.",
